  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nThe weather pattern will shift over the weekend into early next\nweek as the subtropical ridge once again becomes the dominant \nweather feature across the Southwestern U.S. The Pacific trough \nwill somewhat stick around into Saturday, but it is shown to \nquickly lift to the north by Sunday as the ridge moves back in \nfrom the east. Forecast H5 heights rise from 588-590dm on Friday \nto 591-594dm Saturday before peaking around 594-596dm during the \nfirst half of next week. \n\nThe dry air and increasing subsidence this weekend should keep all\nbut a few eastern Arizona high terrain showers and storms from \ndeveloping. Temperatures will warm up quickly over the weekend\nwith the expansion of the ridge with NBM forecast highs reaching\n103-106 degrees Saturday and 106-109 degrees Sunday. The drop in\nmoisture will at least push surface dew points well into the 50s\nthis weekend to as low as the upper 40s early next week. This will\nhelp to keep overnight lows close to normals through the weekend\nbefore we heat up further next week. Ensembles eventually show \nthe ridge center shifting directly over Arizona by around Monday, \nlikely staying in place for a few days. The increased subsidence \nfrom the ridge with no meaningful moisture return happening early \nnext week should keep lower desert PoPs below 10% to at most \n10-20% over the far eastern Arizona high terrain.\n\nAs the modestly strong (90-97th percentile of climatology) ridge \nsettles over Arizona during the first half of next week,\ntemperatures are likely to heat up enough to result in Major \nHeatRisk across portions of the lower deserts. The highest\nlikelihood for Major HeatRisk looks to impact the Phoenix metro\nwith daytime highs around 110 degrees and lows around 90 degrees\nstarting Monday. More rural lower desert areas should benefit from\novernight lows within a couple degrees of normals, but with\nsimilar daytime highs. Due to the higher confidence, we have\nissued an Extreme Heat Watch for the Phoenix metro, valid from \nMonday through Wednesday.",
